---
description: Prevent consumer-specific policy from leaking through shared helpers
alwaysApply: true
---

# Shared helper consumer contracts

- Before changing the output or supported inputs of a shared helper, enumerate every
  call site and state the contract each consumer relies on.
- Do not widen a generic helper to implement one consumer's policy when another
  consumer has different validity rules. Introduce a policy-specific helper or a
  typed result with explicit consumer selection instead.
- Add caller-level regressions for every distinct contract. At least one test per
  consumer must exercise the real shared helper; mocking the helper only tests the
  caller's assumptions.
- For destructive safety gates, prove both the protected action and unaffected
  preflight/dry-run paths. Include a realistic fixture for tolerated external state,
  such as an image pull Secret that is absent from a validation cluster.
- State whether a safety decision is atomic or snapshot-based. For a snapshot check,
  document the check/use race and scope every guarantee to the state observed by the
  final check; require admission or other coordination before claiming continuous
  protection against concurrent actors.
- Enumerate known reference forms from the authoritative API. Either support each
  form, including CSI `nodePublishSecretRef`, or make the exclusion explicit in the
  feature contract, tests, and operational claims.
- Treat a green focused integration test as insufficient when shared framework code
  changed. Retain the caller inventory, contract matrix, and cross-consumer test
  results as review evidence.
